      "heading": "Water 7: nobody designed the ship in one head",
      "narration": "Here is the picture to hang all of that on. In Water Seven, the Galley-La yards build a ship as a chain of crews, and it is worth watching how the work is divided. One crew does nothing but shape timber: take a rough log, produce a shaped plank to spec. The next crew never touches a log — they take shaped planks and join them into frames. The crew after that takes frames and assembles them into a section of hull. And so on, up to the rigging. Each crew's output is the next crew's raw material, and each crew's job is narrow, repetitive and unglamorous. Now notice two things about that arrangement. First, no single shipwright is holding the whole ship in their head. Iceburg draws the intent, but the man cutting the fourteenth plank of the morning is not reasoning about seaworthiness; he is producing a plank to a tolerance. Second, none of the individual jobs is clever. The ship is not the product of one genius step; the ship is the product of the arrangement. That is exactly the claim about neural networks. The neuron is trivial — multiply, add, bend. The behaviour comes from the arrangement. And this is not a metaphor that falls apart when you look closely; it is what actually happens inside a trained network, and you can go and look at it. In a vision network trained on photographs, the first layers converge on edge detectors and colour blobs — the timber-shaping crew. The middle layers combine those edges into corners, textures, repeated patterns, wheel-shaped things. The late layers combine those into car, or dog, or face. Nobody labelled an edge. Nobody told the network that wheels exist or that they are relevant to cars. The only instruction the network ever received was a loss on the final answer, and this entire vocabulary of intermediate parts appeared underneath it because the arrangement made it the cheapest way to be right. That is called representation learning, and it is the real prize of this whole part of the course. In classical machine learning you designed the features by hand — you sat down and decided that word counts, or edge histograms, were what mattered, and the model was only as good as your imagination. A deep network learns the intermediate vocabulary itself. And because those learned parts are general, you can take a network trained on one enormous task and reuse its lower layers on a task with almost no data, which is the idea that the last two parts of this course are built on.",
