      "heading": "What goes wrong, and the settings that matter",
      "narration": "Let's finish with the practical part — the things that actually stop a network training, and the settings you have to choose. First, initialization, which sounds like a formality and is not. Never initialize all the weights to zero. If every neuron in a layer starts identical, then every neuron computes the same output, and — this is the fatal part — every neuron receives exactly the same gradient, so they update identically and stay identical forever. Your layer of five hundred neurons is one neuron wearing five hundred hats, and no amount of training breaks the symmetry. So we initialize randomly. But not with any random numbers: the scale matters, because if the variance of the activations grows or shrinks layer by layer, then by layer twenty the signal has either exploded or faded to nothing. The standard answers are He initialization when you are using ReLU, and Xavier or Glorot when you are using tanh. Both simply set the random scale from the number of inputs so the variance stays roughly constant as the signal moves up the stack. Every framework does this for you by default; you should know why. Second, the failure that puzzles people: dead ReLUs. If a unit's input gets pushed far negative, its output is zero, and because ReLU's gradient is zero on that side, the unit receives no gradient — so it can never come back. It is dead for the rest of training. The usual cause is a learning rate high enough to knock units off the cliff in one step, and the usual fixes are lowering the learning rate or switching to Leaky ReLU, which lets a small negative slope through so there is always some gradient. Third, the output layer, which is chosen by the task and not by taste. Predicting a number? No activation at all on the last layer, plain linear output, and mean squared error. Two classes? One unit with a sigmoid, and binary cross-entropy. More than two classes? One unit per class, a softmax across them so they sum to one, and cross-entropy. Those three pairings are fixed, and if you find yourself inventing a fourth, check the task again. And finally, sizing, which is far less mysterious than it looks. Start with two or three hidden layers a few hundred units wide. Then before you tune anything at all, take a tiny subset of your data — a few dozen examples — and check that your network can overfit it completely, driving the training loss to nearly zero. That is not a good model; it is a test of your plumbing. If a network cannot memorize forty examples, something is wrong with your data pipeline or your loss, and no amount of architecture tuning will save you. Once it passes that test, add the regularization and the real data, and only then start caring about size.",
